      "q": "3. Answer the following questions in about 150 words.\n(i) Describe the composition of export and import trade of India.\nExports:\nOver time, the proportion of agricultural products in India\u2019s exports has reduced, with traditional exports like tea, coffee, and spices decreasing. Petroleum products have gained importance due to rising oil prices and improved refining capabilities. The share of minerals and manufactured goods remains stable, while exports of fruits, seafood, and sugar have increased. Engineering goods now form the biggest portion of exports.\nImports:\nIn earlier decades, food grains dominated imports due to domestic shortages, but after the Green Revolution, their import reduced. Today, petroleum, fertilizers, machinery, specialized steel, chemicals, and edible oils are key imports. Capital goods, such as industrial equipment and transport machinery, have also seen a rise, reflecting growing industrialization and improved living standards. Imports of precious metals and special steel alloys continue to be significant.\n(ii) Write a note on the changing nature of the international trade of India.\nIndia has taken major steps to enhance its participation in global trade, such as lowering import duties, relaxing regulations, and adopting product patent laws. The country trades widely, especially with Asia and Oceania, which together account for nearly half its exports. Europe and America are also significant partners.\nIndia\u2019s exports are shifting toward services and information technology, with software and knowledge-based products playing a vital role in earning foreign exchange. Tourism also contributes to trade. However, the country faces challenges in expanding exports due to slow production growth, higher domestic consumption, and stronger international competition.\nWhy NCERT Solutions for Class 12 Geography Chapter 8 International Trade are Essential\nMastering\nInternational Trade\nopens doors to understanding how economies connect globally. With NCERT solutions, students can clarify tough concepts and prepare confidently for the 2026-27 exams.\nThe chapter gives insight into\ntrade systems, regional blocs, and world organizations\n, building a strong foundation in economic geography. Regular revision ensures conceptual clarity and boosts retention for scoring higher marks.\nPractice with\nchapter-wise solutions\nsharpens your exam strategy. Focus on key terms like balance of trade, trading blocs, and port types for quick revision before your Geography board exams.\nCBSE Class 12 Geography (India-People and Economy) Chapter-wise NCERT Solutions\nS.No\nChapter-wise NCERT Solutions for Geography (India-People and Economy) Class 12\n1\nChapter 1 Population Distribution Density Growth and Composition Solutions\n2\nChapter 2 Human Settlements Solutions\n3\nChapter 3 Land Resources and Agriculture Solutions\n4\nChapter 4 Water Resources Solutions\n5\nChapter 5 Mineral and Energy Resources Solutions\n6\nChapter 6 Planning and Sustainable Development in Indian Context Solutions\n7\nChapter 7 Transport and Communication Solutions\n8\nChapter 9 Geographical Perspective on Selected Issues and Problems Solutions\nCBSE Class 12 Geography Study Materials\nS.No\nStudy Materials for Class 12 Geography\n1\nCBSE Class 12 Geography NCERT Solutions\n2\nCBSE Class 12 Geography NCERT Book\n3\nCBSE Class 12 Geography PYQP",
